slides.raw_text:
Accessing Nodes in the List
❑We maintain a pointer to the first node in the list.
❑We use the next pointer to access the second node.
❑We continue until we reach the last node in the list.
❑Consider searching a list for a particular person name.
▪
We use a reference variable current to traverse the list.
▪
Continue until name is found or the end of list is reached.
String name = "Tom Jones";
Person current = first;
while (!(current.name.equals(name)) && (current.next != null))
  current = current.next;

Inserting a Node at the Front of a List
❑To insert a node at the front of a list, first link the new node 
to the front node, then reassign the front pointer.
❑Inserting a node into the middle of a list requires more 
processing and will be discussed later.
Person node = new Person();
node.next = front;
front = node;

Deleting the Front Node of a List
❑To delete the front node, reassign the front reference to 
point to the next node in the list.
❑To delete a node in the middle of the list requires more 
processing and will be discussed later.
Person next = front.next;
front.next = null;
front = next;

